DAY 27 – DATA ABSTRACTION

1. DATA ABSTRACTION

Data Abstraction means showing what an object does
while hiding how it works internally.

It keeps complex internal logic hidden and provides
a simple and clear interface to the user.


2. ABSTRACT CLASSES

An Abstract Class is used as a blueprint for
child classes.

ABC is used to create abstract classes.

An abstract class defines the structure that
child classes should follow.


3. ABSTRACT METHODS

An Abstract Method is a method that must be
implemented by the child classes.

The @abstractmethod decorator is used to define
an abstract method.

It ensures that required methods are implemented
in child classes.


4. CONCRETE METHODS

A Concrete Method is a method with a complete
implementation.

An abstract class can also contain concrete methods
along with abstract methods.


5. INSTANCE METHODS

Instance methods work with objects.

They use self to access and work with object data.


6. ATM EXAMPLE

An ATM provides a simple interface to the user.

The user can perform operations such as:

• Check balance
• Deposit
• Withdraw

The user can perform these operations without
knowing the internal logic behind them.


KEY TAKEAWAY

Data Abstraction → Shows what an object does while
hiding how it works internally.

Abstract Class → Provides a blueprint for child classes.

Abstract Method → Ensures required methods are
implemented by child classes.

Concrete Method → Contains a complete implementation.

Instance Method → Works with objects using self.

Abstraction keeps things simple for the user by
hiding unnecessary complexity.